Skip the redundant main-thread zeroing of CPU tile buffers during scroll.

CoordinatedUnacceleratedTileBuffer allocates its pixel store with
MallocSpan::tryZeroedMalloc - a full memset of the tile (256x256x4 = 256 KB
each) on the MAIN thread, inside the tile-record path (SkiaPaintingEngine::
replay -> createBuffer). But every tile is fully cleared and painted before it
is ever composited: the raster task does canvas->clear(kTransparent) then plays
back the recording, on a Skia WORKER thread. So the main-thread zero is pure
redundant work.

Device profiling (build 512, gdb main-thread sampling during a sustained
franceinfo scroll) found this memset is the single largest main-thread cost
during scroll - ~30% of CPU-tip samples and present in ~46% of stacks, ahead of
style resolution and layout. The freeze is main-thread-bound (WebProcess main
92% CPU, ThreadedCompositor thread only 12%, Skia workers ~1.5%), so removing
this memset directly attacks the freeze - unlike the low-res tile ladder, which
shrinks worker raster but not main-thread record/alloc, or damage-limited
compositing, which only touches the (idle) compositor thread.

WEBKIT_TILE_BUFFER_SKIP_ZERO=1 switches the allocation to the non-zeroed
MallocSpan::tryMalloc. Default OFF (stock zeroed behaviour). The one code path
that does not overwrite the whole buffer is a failed Skia surface creation
(OOM: canvas() returns null, the paint is skipped), which would then composite
one uninitialised tile - so this is an off-by-default A/B knob that must be
device-verified for garbage flashes before it is ever shipped on.
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/texmap/coordinated/CoordinatedTileBuffer.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/texmap/coordinated/CoordinatedTileBuffer.cpp
@@ -29,6 +29,8 @@
 #include "config.h"
 #include "CoordinatedTileBuffer.h"
 
+#include <cstdlib>
+
 #if USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
 
 #if USE(SKIA)
@@ -130,7 +132,22 @@
     , m_size(size)
 {
     const auto checkedArea = size.area() * 4;
-    m_data = MallocSpan<unsigned char>::tryZeroedMalloc(checkedArea);
+    // Atlantic: the tile pixels are fully overwritten before the buffer is ever
+    // composited - the Skia raster task clears the whole canvas (canvas->clear)
+    // and paints it, on a worker thread - so zeroing here, on the MAIN thread in
+    // the tile-record path, is redundant. Device profiling of franceinfo scroll
+    // showed this memset as the #1 main-thread hot spot (~30% of samples), i.e. a
+    // direct contributor to the scroll freeze (main thread was 92% busy while the
+    // compositor sat at 12%). WEBKIT_TILE_BUFFER_SKIP_ZERO=1 uses a non-zeroed
+    // allocation. Default = stock (zeroed): the only uncovered path is a failed
+    // Skia surface creation (OOM), which would leave one uninitialised tile, so
+    // this must be device-verified for garbage flashes before shipping on.
+    static const bool skipZero = [] {
+        const char* v = getenv("WEBKIT_TILE_BUFFER_SKIP_ZERO");
+        return v && *v && *v != '0';
+    }();
+    m_data = skipZero ? MallocSpan<unsigned char>::tryMalloc(checkedArea)
+        : MallocSpan<unsigned char>::tryZeroedMalloc(checkedArea);
 
     {
         Locker locker { s_layersMemoryUsageLock };
